Project Brief: A Cross-Platform Digital Book Reader and Listening App

1. Product Vision

Build a polished, modern digital reading application inspired by the best experiences in Speechify, 微信读书 (Weixin Dushu), Readwise Reader, and other established reading apps.

The product combines a personal digital library, faithful document rendering, customizable reading, synchronized text-to-speech, and persistent bookmarks, highlights, and notes.

The primary goal is to let users import their own books or paste arbitrary text, then read or listen to that content comfortably across devices.

This is not intended to be a clone of any single existing product. Use established products as inspiration for their user experience and capabilities while developing a coherent, distinctive application.

The product should ultimately support macOS/web and iPhone, with a foundation for future cloud synchronization, AI assistance, premium voices, and community features.

2. Working Principles

* Make sensible engineering and UX decisions independently. Avoid asking for approval for every small implementation detail.
* Prioritize a cohesive, working product over a large collection of disconnected demo features.
* Preserve the original document’s appearance wherever fidelity is important.
* Keep document rendering, speech playback, reading position, annotations, and library management logically independent so that interacting with one does not unnecessarily interrupt another.
* Design for both large and small screens. Do not simply shrink the desktop interface to fit a phone.
* Prefer maintainable, well-supported libraries and standards over reinventing complex document-rendering functionality.
* Avoid unnecessary complexity, premature infrastructure, and overengineering.
* Distinguish implemented functionality from placeholders. Do not represent a disabled or simulated feature as a working capability.
* Make reasonable assumptions where requirements are open-ended and document important trade-offs.

3. Technology Direction

Preferred technologies:

* TypeScript throughout the application where practical.
* React and Vite for the web application.
* React Native with Expo as the preferred direction for a native iPhone application.
* Node.js and TypeScript for the backend.
* TanStack Query for server state, API caching, and request lifecycle management.
* A lightweight client-state solution, such as Zustand or an equivalent, for transient UI and reader state.
* Appropriate local persistence for books, annotations, preferences, and reading progress.
* A relational database and object storage when cloud functionality is introduced.

Choose the most suitable document-rendering and speech technologies based on actual platform capabilities. Candidates include EPUB.js for EPUB rendering and Mozilla PDF.js for PDF rendering.

Do not force EPUB, PDF, and plain text into a single rendering approach.

The first stage may prioritize a responsive web application that works well in desktop browsers on macOS and mobile browsers. Structure the codebase so a native iPhone application can follow without duplicating all business logic.

Before choosing a monorepo structure, backend framework, database, or additional dependencies, inspect the existing repository and select an approach proportional to the project.

4. Stage One: Build a Functional Reading Prototype

Stage One is the primary implementation target. Deliver a complete, usable reading workflow rather than merely creating a UI mockup.

A. Library and book import

Users should be able to:

* Upload multiple files in one operation from their local device.
* Import at least TXT, EPUB, and PDF files.
* Paste arbitrary text into an input area and turn it into a temporary book.
* Import books through a clear, discoverable workflow, including appropriate drag-and-drop support on desktop.
* See each imported book appear on the shelf immediately, with a pending, processing, completed, or failed state as appropriate.
* Track progress independently for each file during multi-file import.
* Open successfully imported books and understand why an unsupported or invalid file failed.
* Rename a book, delete it from the library, and change its cover using an image selected by the user.
* View available book metadata and correct it when appropriate.
* Avoid duplicate library entries when the same content is imported more than once, with a reasonable option to keep or replace an existing copy.

Pasted text should become a first-class, reflowable book that can be read, annotated, bookmarked, and listened to just like an imported text book.

Temporary books should have clear lifecycle behavior. Do not silently discard a user’s content or annotations.

B. Document fidelity and rendering

Treat document fidelity as a core product requirement.

EPUB

Preserve the source book’s structure and styling as far as the format and renderer allow, including chapter hierarchy, headings, paragraph structure, indentation, embedded fonts, images, and distinct heading typography.

Offer optional reader themes and typography adjustments without permanently modifying the original file. Where an override conflicts with the source styling, choose behavior that makes reading comfortable while retaining a way to restore the original appearance.

PDF

Display the original PDF pages, preserving page geometry, images, columns, typography, and layout. Do not convert the PDF into a reflowable text document for its primary display.

Support appropriate zoom, page navigation, single-page and double-page viewing where practical, and scrolling. Use a selectable text layer when available so users can select text, highlight passages, and initiate speech.

Recognize that scanned PDFs may require OCR before text selection or synchronized speech becomes available. Handle that limitation honestly and leave room for a future OCR capability.

TXT and pasted text

Provide a clean, reflowable reading experience with customizable typography, paragraph spacing, line height, and themes.

Design the document layer so additional formats, including MOBI or other supported e-book formats, can be introduced later through format-specific importers or conversion pipelines. Do not promise perfect fidelity for formats whose original rendering behavior cannot be reliably reproduced.

C. Reader interface and navigation

Create a dedicated reading screen with an uncluttered, book-centered experience.

Support:

* Continuous, smooth scrolling.
* Single-page and double-page modes where suitable.
* Previous-page and next-page navigation, with subtle page-turn animations where appropriate.
* A navigable table of contents, including nested chapters and sections when available.
* A progress slider or equivalent navigation control for quickly moving through a book.
* Useful navigation feedback, such as the current chapter, page where applicable, and overall progress.
* Configurable progress indicators, including percentage, page count, chapter progress, elapsed reading/listening time, and estimated time remaining where meaningful.
* Search within the current book when practical.
* Restoration of the last reading position when the book is reopened.

Keep document location distinct from presentation layout. A page number can change when font size, viewport dimensions, or pagination changes, so do not rely on page numbers alone to represent persistent reading positions.

D. Reading customization

Allow users to customize the reading experience, including:

* Font size.
* Line height.
* Paragraph spacing.
* Background and text color through coordinated themes.
* Light, dark, and other comfortable reading themes.
* Scrolling versus paginated reading.
* Relevant typography options where supported by the document format.

Preserve source formatting by default for formatted documents. Let users choose when to override it.

Provide a way to reset customization to sensible defaults or the original appearance.

E. Bookmarks, highlights, and notes

Support bookmarks and text annotations as core reading features.

Users should be able to:

* Bookmark their current reading location.
* Select text and create a highlight or note.
* Highlight text within a paragraph, across paragraph boundaries, or at the beginning or end of a passage.
* Associate notes with selected passages.
* View bookmarks, highlights, and notes in dedicated navigable lists.
* Jump from an annotation or bookmark to its corresponding location in the book.
* Remove or edit their annotations.
* Navigate among annotations without losing the current reading context unnecessarily.

Establish a reasonable configurable or documented limit for the length of a single highlighted passage or attached note. Handle oversized selections gracefully rather than allowing pathological content to degrade performance.

Use stable document anchors and source-text offsets or equivalent location information. Annotations should survive normal navigation and, where possible, changes to typography and layout.

Design the data model so highlights and notes can eventually be exported, synchronized, or shared independently of the original book.

F. Independent panels and responsive UX

Treat the reading surface, reading customization, navigation and annotation lists, and playback controls as related but independently manageable parts of the experience.

The interface should accommodate at least these conceptual areas:

* Reader and document navigation.
* Reading settings and appearance.
* Table of contents, bookmarks, highlights, and notes.
* Text-to-speech playback controls.

Do not require all panels to remain visible simultaneously.

On desktop, use an appropriate combination of sidebars, collapsible panels, toolbars, and overlays. On iPhone-sized screens, use compact controls, drawers, sheets, or other platform-appropriate navigation patterns.

Users should be able to open a panel, inspect or edit its contents, and return to reading without losing their place or unnecessarily interrupting speech.

Long lists of chapters, bookmarks, and annotations should navigate intelligently. When opening a list, bring the entry nearest to the current reading location into view when practical, while keeping other entries discoverable.

An important interaction: if speech is playing from one location and the user browses elsewhere in the book, provide an obvious way to return to the active speech position, such as a “Back to reader” or “Return to current reading” action.

Distinguish the visible browsing location from the active playback location. Moving the reading viewport must not silently change playback, and initiating playback at a new location must deliberately update the active reading position.

Make panel transitions, navigation, and reading interactions smooth and predictable without excessive animation.

G. Text-to-speech and listening

Provide a functional text-to-speech experience in Stage One.

Include:

* Play and pause controls.
* Resume from the saved position.
* A reasonable initial free voice using a supported speech engine.
* Voice selection where multiple usable voices are available.
* Playback speed selection.
* Previous-sentence and next-sentence navigation.
* Starting playback from a selected passage or the user’s chosen text position.
* Automatic continuation through the book where the speech engine supports it.
* Persistent per-book playback position and voice/speed preferences.
* Appropriate playback state indicators.
* Sentence-level highlighting synchronized with speech where the engine provides sufficient timing information.
* Word or character-level highlighting when reliable timing events are available.

Use the first sentence or beginning of the book when the user starts playback for the first time without a specified position. Resume from the last saved position on subsequent sessions unless the user explicitly chooses another starting location.

If a user starts playback from another location while speech is already playing, stop or replace the previous playback cleanly and begin at the new location.

Users must be able to create bookmarks, highlights, and notes while speech continues. These interactions must not unnecessarily interrupt or reset playback.

Account for language differences and mixed-language books. Detect the likely language of the text, preferably at sentence or segment level where appropriate, and use a compatible voice when possible. A language indicator or flag may be used as a helpful UI cue, but do not assume that an entire book has only one language.

Be transparent about browser and operating-system speech limitations. Do not simulate accurate word synchronization with unreliable timing estimates.

Design a speech-provider abstraction so future voice providers can be integrated without rewriting the reader.

H. Persistence and reliability

The prototype must preserve the user’s library, reading progress, preferences, bookmarks, highlights, and notes across ordinary refreshes and application restarts.

Choose appropriate local persistence for the platform and size of the content. Avoid treating browser memory as durable storage.

Provide clear handling for import failures, corrupted files, unavailable voices, interrupted processing, and missing document text.

Do not lose existing annotations merely because the user changes font size, changes a theme, switches reading modes, or navigates between panels.

5. Stage Two: Native Mobile and Production-Quality Reading

After the core web workflow works, refine it and implement the native iPhone experience.

Priorities include:

* Native file importing and the iOS share sheet where feasible.
* A reading interface optimized for touch and small screens.
* Reliable background audio, lock-screen controls, headphone controls, and audio interruption handling where supported.
* Better sentence and word synchronization through suitable speech engines.
* More robust EPUB and PDF selection and annotation behavior.
* Offline reading and durable local storage.
* Import recovery, duplicate management, accessibility, and keyboard/screen-reader support.
* Improved performance for long books and large PDFs.
* Automated testing across representative file formats and viewport sizes.

Reuse shared domain logic and types between web and mobile where beneficial, but do not force platform-specific interactions into a lowest-common-denominator interface.

Do not mark this stage complete solely because the app runs in a mobile browser. The eventual native iPhone experience must be evaluated on its own platform.

6. Stage Three: Cloud Library and Cross-Device Synchronization

Design for cloud functionality from the beginning, but defer a full community catalog and production cloud service until the core reader is stable.

The future architecture should accommodate:

* User accounts and authentication.
* Private cloud storage for imported books.
* Cross-device synchronization of library metadata, reading positions, preferences, bookmarks, highlights, and notes.
* Upload and download status, conflict handling, and recovery from interrupted transfers.
* Book metadata and version management.
* Optional sharing of selected books and associated annotations.
* Searching for books that have been shared with the user or made available through a permitted catalog.
* Downloading a shared book to a local library.
* Clear controls for private, shared, and public content.

Separate a user’s library entry from the underlying stored file and from any community catalog entry.

Deleting a book from a personal library should not automatically delete a shared community copy or another user’s independent library entry. Use explicit ownership, references, and retention rules to manage stored files.

When replacing an uploaded book, validate the new version before switching the user’s active copy. Preserve or discard older versions according to a clear retention policy.

Cloud upload should not imply public sharing. Community participation must be explicit, with privacy controls and an appropriate copyright and takedown process.

Allow community annotations to be shared separately from the book. Consider annotation ownership, attribution, visibility, edit permissions, and whether an annotation refers to a particular book version.

Implement the architecture needed to support these concepts without building the entire cloud-sharing product in Stage One.

7. Stage Four: AI and Advanced Monetization

Leave clean integration points for future features such as:

* AI explanations of selected text.
* Contextual questions about a book or passage.
* Summaries and chapter explanations.
* More natural or specialized speech voices.
* Premium voices, usage-based credits, or in-app purchases where supported.
* User dictionaries and pronunciation corrections.
* OCR and additional document-format support.
* Advanced reading analytics and exportable annotations.

Future-only features should be visibly labeled as coming soon and should not expose nonfunctional controls that appear to work.

Do not require paid AI services or premium voice APIs to demonstrate the Stage One prototype. Use free, local, browser-native, or otherwise appropriately available capabilities where practical, while keeping provider-specific code isolated.

8. Design and Product Quality

Aim for a polished, calm, reading-first interface rather than a generic dashboard.

The library should feel like a personal bookshelf. The reader should give the text room to breathe. Controls should be easy to discover without competing with the book for attention.

Use thoughtful typography, spacing, clear hierarchy, restrained animation, meaningful empty states, and helpful error messages.

Make the most common actions straightforward: import a book, open it, resume reading, start listening, save a passage, and return to the current reading position.

Avoid excessive settings on the main reading surface. Place secondary controls in well-organized panels.

Keep the desktop and mobile experiences visually related but interaction-appropriate for their platforms.

9. Engineering Expectations

Before implementation:

1. Inspect the existing repository, package manager, scripts, and current application structure.
2. Identify existing functionality worth preserving.
3. Select a reasonable architecture and document important decisions.
4. Establish a representative test corpus or fixtures for TXT, EPUB, PDF, and mixed-language text.

During implementation:

* Work in small, coherent stages.
* Use strict TypeScript where practical.
* Keep document rendering, speech playback, annotations, library management, and UI state separated into understandable modules.
* Define stable domain models for books, document locations, reading progress, bookmarks, highlights, notes, playback preferences, and future cloud references.
* Handle loading, empty, error, and recovery states.
* Include automated tests for important logic and user workflows.
* Avoid unnecessary dependencies and excessive abstractions.
* Do not leave core Stage One actions as static buttons or fake interactions.
* Use realistic sample content and verify actual file rendering rather than relying exclusively on mock data.

At the end of each stage, report what was implemented, what remains incomplete, important limitations, and how to run and test the application.

10. Definition of Done for Stage One

The first stage should be considered successful when a user can:

1. Import several supported books and see each file’s processing status independently.
2. Paste text and create a usable temporary book.
3. Open EPUBs while retaining their formatting and view PDFs in their original page layout.
4. Adjust supported reading settings and navigate chapters and pages.
5. Save bookmarks, highlights, and notes, then navigate back to them.
6. Start, pause, resume, and reposition text-to-speech playback.
7. See synchronized sentence highlighting when supported by the speech engine.
8. Continue using reading controls and annotations without unnecessarily interrupting speech.
9. Return to the active playback position after browsing another location.
10. Close and reopen the application without losing the library, annotations, preferences, or reading progress.
11. Use the responsive web application comfortably on desktop and mobile-sized screens.
12. Run the project and its relevant tests using documented instructions.

Treat unsupported capabilities honestly and document them rather than claiming full compatibility.

Final Instruction

Begin by examining the existing project and proposing a concise implementation plan for Stage One, including key architectural decisions and risks. Then proceed with implementation in coherent increments, validating each increment before moving on.

Use your judgment to fill gaps in the product specification. Prioritize the most valuable working end-to-end experience, preserve the flexibility needed for later stages, and avoid implementing future-stage complexity prematurely.
